1
00:00:00,020 --> 00:00:02,600
I'm V Varadharajan, and this is Faultline.

2
00:00:03,240 --> 00:00:05,020
It does two jobs on one engine.

3
00:00:05,320 --> 00:00:07,800
Ask a question, and it answers

4
00:00:08,320 --> 00:00:10,960
from the literature. Or hand it your
draft,

5
00:00:10,960 --> 00:00:13,600
and three independent reviewers attack it
before

6
00:00:14,220 --> 00:00:16,680
a real referee does. Seven model lineages,

7
00:00:17,280 --> 00:00:19,420
in opposed roles. Here is the second job,

8
00:00:19,940 --> 00:00:24,500
on a real paper. This is SEVA, a detector
for corpus

9
00:00:25,020 --> 00:00:27,600
poisoning in retrieval-augmented
generation. Eight months of work, going

10
00:00:27,600 --> 00:00:30,180
to IEEE Transactions on Dependable and
Secure Computing.

11
00:00:30,760 --> 00:00:32,200
Nobody has

12
00:00:32,580 --> 00:00:35,510
refereed it yet. It reads ten thousand
words, pulls

13
00:00:35,510 --> 00:00:38,440
out nine empirical claims, and identifies
the field

14
00:00:39,180 --> 00:00:42,560
on its own. Then three reviewers attack
it,

15
00:00:42,880 --> 00:00:44,580
each on a different model family,

16
00:00:45,140 --> 00:00:46,660
so they do not share blind spots.

17
00:00:47,120 --> 00:00:49,440
Six major objections.

18
00:00:49,760 --> 00:00:53,070
The method reviewer went at the headline
number. The paper reports

19
00:00:53,070 --> 00:00:56,380
zero percent poison evasion with a
ninety-five percent Wilson bound,

20
00:00:56,640 --> 00:00:59,420
and asked for the base rate,

21
00:00:59,880 --> 00:01:02,360
because without it that bound does not
mean what

22
00:01:02,920 --> 00:01:05,000
it looks like. The sharpest one

23
00:01:05,440 --> 00:01:07,820
questions the title itself. SEVA claims to
be LLM-free,

24
00:01:08,400 --> 00:01:12,460
but it depends on pretrained embedding
models. A referee can

25
00:01:13,080 --> 00:01:15,560
call that external model dependence under
another name.

26
00:01:16,360 --> 00:01:19,820
Now the other job, in a completely
different field.

27
00:01:20,420 --> 00:01:22,520
Does creatine improve cognitive
performance in healthy adults?

28
00:01:22,760 --> 00:01:24,380
It searches four

29
00:01:24,700 --> 00:01:27,060
databases at once, screens twenty-nine
papers,

30
00:01:27,740 --> 00:01:29,300
reads the ones that survive,

31
00:01:29,580 --> 00:01:31,580
and answers. Creatine modestly helps,

32
00:01:32,100 --> 00:01:34,700
especially during sleep deprivation.
Moderate confidence, qualified consensus.

33
00:01:35,280 --> 00:01:37,540
But the answer alone is not the useful
part.

34
00:01:38,380 --> 00:01:40,740
Underneath it are five

35
00:01:41,040 --> 00:01:43,320
conditions that change it, each tagged
with

36
00:01:43,920 --> 00:01:47,320
the axis it moves on. Population. Dose,
and the benefit is dose-dependent.

37
00:01:47,960 --> 00:01:51,480
Setting, because most of the effect
appears under sleep deprivation.

38
00:01:52,100 --> 00:01:56,240
Duration. And measurement, because the
studies used different tests.

39
00:01:56,860 --> 00:01:59,240
Every finding names the paper it came
from,

40
00:01:59,540 --> 00:02:01,000
and it shows you the denominator:

41
00:02:01,500 --> 00:02:02,720
thirty-five retrieved,

42
00:02:02,960 --> 00:02:05,180
twenty-nine screened, and how many
survived.

43
00:02:05,860 --> 00:02:09,280
A chat model gives you a handful of papers
and

44
00:02:09,900 --> 00:02:10,900
no

45
00:02:11,040 --> 00:02:12,760
idea what it missed.

46
00:02:13,140 --> 00:02:15,780
The stack. LangGraph for orchestration.
Seven model lineages across Ollama
locally,

47
00:02:16,080 --> 00:02:18,440
plus Groq and OpenRouter free tiers.

48
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:21,680
Retrieval spans OpenAlex, Crossref, Europe
PMC and arXiv.

49
00:02:21,960 --> 00:02:24,440
The paper review took a hundred

50
00:02:25,060 --> 00:02:27,600
and thirteen seconds. The question took
forty-four.

51
00:02:28,240 --> 00:02:29,720
Over ninety percent ran

52
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:33,420
locally on one consumer GPU. Total cost:
zero dollars. Faultline.

53
00:02:34,080 --> 00:02:38,780
Built by V Varadharajan and A Sowmiya
Priya, from SRM. It reviews the paper

54
00:02:39,260 --> 00:02:41,480
you are too close to read.
